    "result_text": "The build is green. `AcceptanceTest` is the only test class in the project, and it passes along with a full `mvn test` run.\n\n## Summary\n\nI replaced the string membership tier with a numeric `membershipLevel` (1\u20133):\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 Replaced the `membershipTier` schema property (string, `GOLD`/`SILVER`/`BRONZE`) with `membershipLevel` (`integer`, `minimum: 1`, `maximum: 3`), so the generated `OwnerDto` now exposes `membershipLevel` instead of `membershipTier`.\n\n2. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 Rewrote the MapStruct mapping to compute the level: `min(3, 1 + (email present ? 1 : 0) + (namesakeCount == 0 ? 1 : 0))`. Start at 1, +1 for an email, +1 for `namesakeCount == 0`, capped at 3 (leaving 4 for tenure).\n\n3. **`src/main/java/.../controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 Updated the now-stale comment that referred to `householdSize` deriving the \"GOLD membership tier.\"\n\nOn the spec note that \"anything that recorded the tier (such as the create audit line) must record the level instead\": the create audit line in this codebase logs only `id`, `customerCode`, and `registrationDate` \u2014 it never recorded the tier, and there is no other place that persisted or logged it (only the mapper derived it for the response). So there was nothing recording the tier to convert; the tier existed purely as a derived response field, which is now `membershipLevel`.\n\nThe spec's example case (unique owner with email \u2192 `1 + email + namesake0 = 3`) returns `membershipLevel: 3` as asserted by the test.",
